// =============================================================================
// 🔐 Firestore Security Rules — deny all direct client access
// =============================================================================
// Your frontend (Sales-Forecast-app.html / dashboard.html) only ever uses
// the Firebase client SDK for AUTHENTICATION (login/signup). It never reads
// or writes Firestore directly — every read/write in this app goes through
// your FastAPI backend, which uses the Firebase ADMIN SDK.
//
// The Admin SDK always bypasses these rules entirely, running with full
// trusted access — so it keeps working no matter what you put here. These
// rules only ever apply to CLIENT-side requests (web/mobile apps using
// firebase.firestore()).
//
// Since nothing legitimate ever needs client-side Firestore access, the
// correct and simplest rule is to deny it completely. This closes the
// exact hole you were right to worry about: your Firebase web config
// (apiKey, project id, etc.) is public in the page source by design —
// that's normal and fine for Firebase Auth — but if Firestore rules were
// left at their default "test mode" (allow read, write: if true;) or
// anything permissive, anyone could open the browser console, call
// firebase.firestore(), and read or overwrite every user's models,
// forecasts, usage counters and subscription status directly, completely
// bypassing your API, your quota checks, and your billing.
//
// HOW TO APPLY THIS:
//   Firebase Console -> Firestore Database -> Rules tab -> paste this in
//   -> Publish. (Or via CLI: `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` if
//   you have the Firebase CLI + firebase.json set up.)
//
// If you ever DO want a client to read its own data directly (skipping
// your API), replace the relevant block below with a scoped rule like:
//   match /users/{uid} {
//     allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
//   }
// but you do not need that for how this app currently works — the API
// already handles all reads/writes with the Admin SDK.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Deny everything by default. Only the Admin SDK (used by your FastAPI
    // backend) can read or write — it bypasses these rules entirely.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
